From 447a0bb1dd6a2420b33504d7fe9d71c10367c0d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:02:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 026/240] Bugfix: do not overflow the strategy-string buffers
 with large ratios [report R. Perry]

The predefined strategy builders formatted their imbalance and density ratios
into small fixed stack buffers with `sprintf (buf, "%lf", val)'. A value such as
the `1e300' that some callers use as a "no constraint" sentinel expands to more
than three hundred characters and smashed the stack (stack-buffer-overflow
confirmed with AddressSanitizer for the sequential and the parallel builders);
even `1e30' overflowed the 32-byte buffers. Non-finite values silently produced
unparsable `nan'/`inf' tokens.

Reject non-finite and negative ratios up front, as the `gmap'/`dgmap' tools
already do for their `-b' option, and bound each conversion with `snprintf()',
returning the documented error value `1' when the ratio does not fit. Valid
ratios keep their exact `"%lf"' textual form. All eight sequential and parallel
map, cluster, overlap-partitioning and ordering builders are fixed, and
`test_strat_seq' / `test_strat_par' gain a rejection check.

Refs: API-07
---
